{"34429":{"aka":["Clifton School","Clifton Village Hall"],"y":1871,"f":"exact","src":"OpenStreetMap","h":10,"osm":"way/103086209","kind":"public","listEntry":1255222,"listUrl":"https://historicengland.org.uk/listing/the-list/list-entry/1255222","qid":"Q26546812","image":"http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Village%20Hall%2C%20Clifton.jpg","cat":"Clifton Village Hall, Nottinghamshire","stories":[{"layer":"clifton","text":"Henry Robert Clifton laid the school's cornerstone on 3 November 1871, and it opened in April 1872 with 48 children; a night school for men began a year later. It closed in 1956, when new schools were built on the estate, and is now the village hall.","source":{"title":"7A. Clifton Village Appendix (transportnottingham.com)","url":"https://www.transportnottingham.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/08/7A.-Clifton-Village-Appendix.pdf","kind":"transportnottingham.com","read":"2026-11-16"},"when":"1871","whenSrc":{"title":"7A. Clifton Village Appendix (transportnottingham.com)","url":"https://www.transportnottingham.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/08/7A.-Clifton-Village-Appendix.pdf","kind":"transportnottingham.com","read":"2026-11-16"},"nm":"Clifton village school"}],"sum":"Clifton village school is a community centre on Village Road. The building was built in 1871, according to OpenStreetMap. It is Grade II listed as Clifton School.","sumSrc":{"title":"OpenStreetMap · Historic England","kind":"Written from the map's data"},"council":"Nottingham City Council","xy":[-1.18702,52.90838]},"73894":{"y":1877,"f":"exact","src":"OpenStreetMap","h":10,"osm":"way/291697300","kind":"commercial","listEntry":1270498,"listUrl":"https://historicengland.org.uk/listing/the-list/list-entry/1270498","qid":"Q6744638","image":"http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Malt%20Cross%20Upstairs.jpg","article":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Malt_Cross","architects":[["Edwin Hill","https://leftlion.co.uk/features/2014/11/the-history-of-the-malt-cross-nottingham/"]],"stories":[{"layer":"market","text":"The site was once an inn called the Roebuck. In 1877 a music hall was built here, to designs credited to Edwin Hill, under a high arched glass roof on laminated wooden arches. It took its name from a monument that once stood in the market square, and it is one of the few Victorian music halls to survive.","source":{"title":"Malt Cross","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Malt_Cross","kind":"Wikipedia"},"when":"1877","whenSrc":{"title":"Malt Cross","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Malt_Cross","kind":"Wikipedia"},"nm":"Malt Cross"},{"layer":"people","tpl":"architect-osm","who":"Edwin Hill","source":{"title":"OpenStreetMap","url":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/way/291697300","kind":"OpenStreetMap"}}],"sum":"The Malt Cross is a building in Nottingham, England. It is situated on St James Street in Nottingham city centre, off the Old Market Square. The building was built in 1877 and is one of only a few Victorian music halls still standing.","sumSrc":{"title":"Malt Cross","kind":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Malt_Cross"},"open":[{"name":"Malt Cross","what":"pub","web":"https://www.maltcross.com/"}],"council":"Nottingham City Council","coll":[{"title":"Old Market Square","url":"https://picturenottingham.co.uk/image-themes/nottinghams-old-market-square.html"}],"xy":[-1.15246,52.95313]},"73964":{"y":1827,"f":"exact","src":"OpenStreetMap","h":8,"osm":"way/168974350","kind":"residential","listEntry":1254871,"listUrl":"https://historicengland.org.uk/listing/the-list/list-entry/1254871","qid":"Q26681761","image":"http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/The_Tower_House_-_geograph.org.uk_-_8334230.jpg","credit":{"file":"The Tower House - geograph.org.uk - 8334230.jpg","year":2026,"author":"John Sutton","licence":"CC BY-SA 2.0"},"archive":[{"file":"Looking up Park Steps, 1967 - geograph.org.uk - 1612802.jpg","year":1967,"author":"John Sutton","licence":"CC BY-SA 2.0"}],"architects":[["Watson Fothergill","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Watson_Fothergill"]],"cat":"Tower House, Park Row, Nottingham","stories":[{"layer":"park","text":"Tower House is one of the earliest houses in the Park, built to P. F. Robinson's designs between 1827 and 1833.[^walk] Watson Fothergill later gave it the tower that names it.[^walk] It is listed Grade II.","source":{"title":"The Park Estate","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Park_Estate","kind":"Wikipedia"},"cites":{"walk":{"title":"2A. The Park Estate and Nottingham–Beeston Canal Appendix (transportnottingham.com)","url":"https://www.transportnottingham.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/08/2A.-The-Park-Estate-Nottm-Bton-Canal-Appendix.pdf","kind":"transportnottingham.com","read":"2026-11-16"}},"weblinks":[["Watson Fothergill","note:Watson Fothergill"]],"links":["Watson Fothergill"],"when":"1827","whenSrc":{"title":"The Park Estate","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Park_Estate","kind":"Wikipedia"},"nm":"Tower House"},{"layer":"people","tpl":"architect","who":"Watson Fothergill","source":{"title":"Watson Fothergill","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Watson_Fothergill","kind":"Wikipedia"}}],"sum":"Tower House is a residential building on Park Row. The building was built in 1827, according to OpenStreetMap. It is Grade II listed and was designed by Watson Fothergill.","sumSrc":{"title":"OpenStreetMap · Historic England · Wikidata","kind":"Written from the map's data"},"council":"Nottingham City Council","coll":[{"title":"Nottingham Castle","url":"https://picturenottingham.co.uk/image-themes/nottingham-castle.html"}],"xy":[-1.15661,52.95174]},"74534":{"y":1855,"f":"exact","src":"Historic England (via Wikipedia)","dt":"1855","h":10,"osm":"relation/3581525","kind":"college","listEntry":1270430,"listUrl":"https://historicengland.org.uk/listing/the-list/list-entry/1270430","qid":"Q4680301","image":"http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Adams%20Building%2C%20Lace%20Market%20-%20geograph.org.uk%20-%20319228.jpg","article":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adams_Building,_Nottingham","architects":[["Thomas Chambers Hine","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Chambers_Hine"]],"cat":"Adams Building, Nottingham","stories":[{"layer":"lacemarket","text":"Built from 1854 to designs by Thomas Chambers Hine for Thomas Adams, a Quaker lace manufacturer, and opened in 1855 as his lace showroom and warehouse, with a chapel for the workers in the basement. Inspectors thought it a model example of its kind; since 1999 it has been part of Nottingham College.","source":{"title":"Adams Building, Nottingham","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adams_Building,_Nottingham","kind":"Wikipedia"},"weblinks":[["Thomas Chambers Hine","note:Thomas Chambers Hine"],["Thomas Adams","note:Thomas Adams"]],"links":["Thomas Chambers Hine","Thomas Adams"],"when":"1855","whenSrc":{"title":"Adams Building, Nottingham","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adams_Building,_Nottingham","kind":"Wikipedia"},"nm":"Adams Building"},{"layer":"people","text":"The lace manufacturer Thomas Adams built this showroom and warehouse; it is named after him.[^s1][^s2]","source":{"title":"Thomas Adams (manufacturer and philanthropist)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Adams_(manufacturer_and_philanthropist)","kind":"Wikipedia"},"who":"Thomas Adams","sources":[{"title":"Adams Building, Nottingham","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adams_Building,_Nottingham","kind":"Wikipedia"}],"cites":{"s1":{"title":"Thomas Adams (manufacturer and philanthropist)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Adams_(manufacturer_and_philanthropist)","kind":"Wikipedia"},"s2":{"title":"Adams Building, Nottingham","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adams_Building,_Nottingham","kind":"Wikipedia"}},"when":"1807","whenSrc":{"title":"Thomas Adams (manufacturer and philanthropist)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Adams_(manufacturer_and_philanthropist)","kind":"Wikipedia"},"own":1},{"layer":"people","text":"The Nottingham architect Thomas Chambers Hine designed this lace showroom and warehouse.[^s1][^s2]","source":{"title":"Thomas Chambers Hine","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Chambers_Hine","kind":"Wikipedia"},"who":"Thomas Chambers Hine","sources":[{"title":"Adams Building, Nottingham","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adams_Building,_Nottingham","kind":"Wikipedia"}],"cites":{"s1":{"title":"Thomas Chambers Hine","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Chambers_Hine","kind":"Wikipedia"},"s2":{"title":"Adams Building, Nottingham","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adams_Building,_Nottingham","kind":"Wikipedia"}},"when":"1813","whenSrc":{"title":"Thomas Chambers Hine","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Chambers_Hine","kind":"Wikipedia"},"own":1}],"sum":"The Adams Building, formerly the Adams and Page warehouse on Stoney Street, is the largest building in the Lace Market district of the city of Nottingham in England. Now Grade II*-listed by Historic England, the Adams Building was formerly a lace showroom and warehouse. Since 1999, it has formed part of the city campus of what is now Nottingham College.","sumSrc":{"title":"Adams Building, Nottingham","kind":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adams_Building%2C_Nottingham"},"council":"Nottingham City Council","coll":[{"title":"The Lace Market","url":"https://picturenottingham.co.uk/image-themes/lace-market.html"}],"xy":[-1.1438,52.95274]},"74693":{"aka":["15 And 17, George Street"],"y":1895,"f":"exact","src":"Historic England (via Wikipedia)","dt":"1895","h":10,"osm":"way/288396586","listEntry":1247548,"listUrl":"https://historicengland.org.uk/listing/the-list/list-entry/1247548","qid":"Q26539851","image":"http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/15%20And%2017%2C%20George%20Street.jpg","article":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Watson_Fothergill's_offices","architects":[["Watson Fothergill","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Watson_Fothergill"]],"cat":"Watson Fothergill's Offices, George Street","stories":[{"layer":"lacemarket","text":"Watson Fothergill, born Fothergill Watson, took his mother's family name in 1892 and built his own offices here, on George Street, in 1895.[^s1] Its front has a statue of a medieval architect and busts of Augustus Pugin and George Edmund Street; it is listed Grade II.[^s2]","source":{"title":"Watson Fothergill's offices","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Watson_Fothergill%27s_offices","kind":"Wikipedia"},"sources":[{"title":"Watson Fothergill","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Watson_Fothergill","kind":"Wikipedia"}],"cites":{"s1":{"title":"Watson Fothergill","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Watson_Fothergill","kind":"Wikipedia"},"s2":{"title":"Watson Fothergill's offices","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Watson_Fothergill%27s_offices","kind":"Wikipedia"}},"weblinks":[["Watson Fothergill","note:Watson Fothergill"]],"links":["Watson Fothergill"],"nm":"Watson Fothergill's offices","see":[["The Black Boy Hotel","p668"]]}],"sum":"Watson Fothergill’s office is a Grade II listed building in George Street, Nottingham.","sumSrc":{"title":"Watson Fothergill's offices","kind":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Watson_Fothergill%27s_offices"},"council":"Nottingham City Council","coll":[{"title":"The Lace Market","url":"https://picturenottingham.co.uk/image-themes/lace-market.html"}],"xy":[-1.14544,52.95449]},"74724":{"today":"Lloyds Bank","aka":["12, 14 And 16, Lower Parliament Street"],"full":"Furley & Co · Lloyds Bank","y":1896,"f":"exact","src":"Historic England (via Wikipedia)","dt":"1896","h":10,"osm":"way/292812434","listEntry":1254750,"listUrl":"https://historicengland.org.uk/listing/the-list/list-entry/1254750","qid":"Q26546393","image":"http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/12-16_Lower_Parliament_Street%2C_Nottingham_-_geograph.org.uk_-_4062266.jpg","credit":{"file":"12-16 Lower Parliament Street, Nottingham - geograph.org.uk - 4062266.jpg","year":2012,"author":"Stephen Richards","licence":"CC BY-SA 2.0"},"stories":[{"layer":"market","text":"Watson Fothergill designed this shop and warehouse for Furley & Co, provision merchants, in 1896–97; Lloyds Bank has it now. Terracotta panels show the sugar trade, from cutting and boiling the cane to boats and a far eastern market, and the Clinton Street side is faced with glazed Art Nouveau tiles.[^trail]","source":{"title":"Watson Fothergill","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Watson_Fothergill","kind":"Wikipedia"},"cites":{"trail":{"title":"Watson Fothergill trail (Carlton U3A)","url":"https://carlton.u3asite.uk/wp-content/uploads/2023/11/trail-watsonfothergill.pdf","kind":"carlton.u3asite.uk","read":"2026-11-26"}},"weblinks":[["Watson Fothergill","note:Watson Fothergill"]],"links":["Watson Fothergill"],"nm":"Furley & Co"}],"sum":"Furley & Co, on Lower Parliament Street. Today it houses Lloyds Bank, a bank. The building was built in 1896, according to Historic England (via Wikipedia). It is Grade II listed as 12, 14 and 16, Lower Parliament Street.","sumSrc":{"title":"OpenStreetMap · Historic England","kind":"Written from the map's data"},"council":"Nottingham City Council","coll":[{"title":"Old Market Square","url":"https://picturenottingham.co.uk/image-themes/nottinghams-old-market-square.html"}],"xy":[-1.14717,52.95497]},"74992":{"y":1865,"f":"exact","src":"OpenStreetMap","h":10,"osm":"way/16464923","kind":"theatre","listEntry":1255233,"listUrl":"https://historicengland.org.uk/listing/the-list/list-entry/1255233","qid":"Q7777438","image":"http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Theatre%20Royal%2C%20Nottingham%20-%20geograph.org.uk%20-%20997109.jpg","article":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theatre_Royal,_Nottingham","architects":[["Charles J. Phipps","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/C._J._Phipps"]],"cat":"Theatre Royal, Nottingham","stories":[{"layer":"market","text":"Built for the lace manufacturers John and William Lambert, who wanted a prestigious theatre for the town, to designs by Charles J. Phipps; its portico of six Corinthian columns closes the view up Market Street. It opened on 25 September 1865 with Sheridan's The School for Scandal, and in 1897 Frank Matcham remodelled its auditorium. In October 1952 it staged the world premiere of Agatha Christie's The Mousetrap.","source":{"title":"Theatre Royal, Nottingham","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theatre_Royal,_Nottingham","kind":"Wikipedia"},"weblinks":[["Charles J. Phipps","note:Charles J. Phipps"]],"links":["Charles J. Phipps"],"when":"1865","whenSrc":{"title":"Theatre Royal, Nottingham","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theatre_Royal,_Nottingham","kind":"Wikipedia"},"nm":"Theatre Royal"},{"layer":"people","text":"The theatre architect Charles J. Phipps designed the Theatre Royal.[^s1][^s2]","source":{"title":"C. J. Phipps","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/C._J._Phipps","kind":"Wikipedia"},"who":"Charles J. Phipps","sources":[{"title":"Theatre Royal, Nottingham","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theatre_Royal,_Nottingham","kind":"Wikipedia"}],"cites":{"s1":{"title":"C. J. Phipps","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/C._J._Phipps","kind":"Wikipedia"},"s2":{"title":"Theatre Royal, Nottingham","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theatre_Royal,_Nottingham","kind":"Wikipedia"}},"when":"1835","whenSrc":{"title":"C. J. Phipps","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/C._J._Phipps","kind":"Wikipedia"},"own":1},{"layer":"people","pin":"p84"},{"layer":"people","tpl":"architect","who":"Charles J. Phipps","source":{"title":"Charles J. Phipps","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/C._J._Phipps","kind":"Wikipedia"}}],"sum":"The Theatre Royal in Nottingham, England, is a theatre venue in the heart of Nottingham City Centre and is owned by Nottingham City Council as part of a complex that also includes the city's Royal Concert Hall. The Theatre Royal attracts major touring dramas, opera, ballet, West End musicals and an annual pantomime.","sumSrc":{"title":"Theatre Royal, Nottingham","kind":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theatre_Royal%2C_Nottingham"},"open":[{"name":"Yarn","what":"pub","web":"https://www.castlerockbrewery.co.uk/pubs/yarn/"}],"council":"Nottingham City Council","coll":[{"title":"Old Market Square","url":"https://picturenottingham.co.uk/image-themes/nottinghams-old-market-square.html"}],"xy":[-1.15132,52.95526]},"74994":{"y":1982,"f":"exact","src":"Wikidata","h":10,"osm":"way/40324339","qid":"Q7063688","image":"http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/The_Royal_Concert_Hall_-_geograph.org.uk_-_7866093.jpg","credit":{"file":"The Royal Concert Hall - geograph.org.uk - 7866093.jpg","year":2024,"author":"John Sutton","licence":"CC BY-SA 2.0"},"article":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nottingham_Royal_Concert_Hall","architects":[["RHWL","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/RHWL"]],"cat":"Nottingham Royal Concert Hall","stories":[{"layer":"market","text":"In 1898 Frank Matcham designed and built the Empire Palace of Varieties here. It closed for the last time in 1958 and was demolished for road-widening in 1969. On 27 November 1982 the Royal Concert Hall, designed by the Renton Howard Wood Levin Partnership, opened on the site, with Elton John as its first performer.","source":{"title":"Nottingham Royal Concert Hall","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nottingham_Royal_Concert_Hall","kind":"Wikipedia"},"when":"1898","made":"1982","whenSrc":{"title":"Nottingham Royal Concert Hall","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nottingham_Royal_Concert_Hall","kind":"Wikipedia"},"madeSrc":{"title":"Nottingham Royal Concert Hall","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nottingham_Royal_Concert_Hall","kind":"Wikipedia"},"nm":"Royal Concert Hall"},{"layer":"people","tpl":"architect","who":"RHWL","source":{"title":"RHWL","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/RHWL","kind":"Wikipedia"}}],"sum":"Nottingham Royal Concert Hall is a concert hall in the English city of Nottingham. It is owned by Nottingham City Council and is part of a complex that also includes the city's Theatre Royal. The Royal Concert Hall's striking modern architecture has proved to be a city landmark at the heart of Nottingham City Centre, opposite the more recently built The Cornerhouse complex.","sumSrc":{"title":"Nottingham Royal Concert Hall","kind":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nottingham_Royal_Concert_Hall"},"council":"Nottingham City Council","xy":[-1.1517,52.95579]},"74995":{"today":"The Guildhall","y":1887,"f":"exact","src":"OpenStreetMap","alts":[{"y":1888,"f":"exact","src":"Wikipedia"},{"y":1888,"f":"exact","src":"Wikidata"}],"h":10,"osm":"way/67922425","kind":"public","listEntry":1246296,"listUrl":"https://historicengland.org.uk/listing/the-list/list-entry/1246296","qid":"Q7063650","image":"http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Nottingham%20Guildhall%20%282%29.JPG","article":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nottingham_Guildhall","architects":[["Thomas Verity","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Verity"]],"cat":"Nottingham Guildhall","stories":[{"layer":"north","text":"Built in 1887–88 to designs by Thomas Verity and George Henry Hunt, chosen in a competition judged by Alfred Waterhouse, it replaced the old Guild Hall on Weekday Cross and served as the city's magistrates' court. The council used it as offices until 2010; it has stood largely empty since.","source":{"title":"Nottingham Guildhall","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nottingham_Guildhall","kind":"Wikipedia"},"when":"1888","whenSrc":{"title":"Nottingham Guildhall","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nottingham_Guildhall","kind":"Wikipedia"},"nm":"Guildhall"},{"layer":"people","tpl":"architect","who":"Thomas Verity","source":{"title":"Thomas Verity","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Verity","kind":"Wikipedia"}}],"sum":"Nottingham Guildhall is a former magistrates' court in Nottingham, England. The structure, which was used by Nottingham City Council as offices in the 1990s, is a Grade II listed building.","sumSrc":{"title":"Nottingham Guildhall","kind":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nottingham_Guildhall"},"council":"Nottingham City Council","xy":[-1.15068,52.95656]},"75086":{"aka":["Victoria Centre Clock Tower","Nottingham Victoria Clock Tower"],"y":1899,"f":"exact","src":"OpenStreetMap","h":38.0,"osm":"way/116816364","listEntry":1270622,"listUrl":"https://historicengland.org.uk/listing/the-list/list-entry/1270622","qid":"Q26560657","image":"http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Clocktower%20-%20panoramio.jpg","architects":[["Albert Edward Lambert","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Albert_Edward_Lambert"]],"cat":"Victoria Centre Clock Tower, Nottingham","stories":[{"layer":"north","text":"Nottingham Victoria, the Great Central and Great Northern railways' station, opened on 24 May 1900 and closed on 4 September 1967. It was demolished except for its clock tower, which still stands at the entrance of the Victoria Centre built on the site.","source":{"title":"Nottingham Victoria railway station","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nottingham_Victoria_railway_station","kind":"Wikipedia"},"nm":"Nottingham Victoria station","see":[["Victoria Centre","75088"]]},{"layer":"people","tpl":"architect-osm","who":"Albert Edward Lambert","source":{"title":"OpenStreetMap","url":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/way/116816364","kind":"OpenStreetMap"}}],"sum":"Nottingham Victoria station: a takeaway. The building was built in 1899, according to OpenStreetMap. It is Grade II listed as Victoria Centre Clock Tower and was designed by Albert Edward Lambert.","sumSrc":{"title":"OpenStreetMap · Historic England","kind":"Written from the map's data"},"council":"Nottingham City Council","xy":[-1.14815,52.95723]},"76264":{"today":"Arkwright","y":1879,"f":"approx","src":"Wikipedia","h":10.6,"levels":"3","osm":"relation/1750480","kind":"university","listEntry":1255017,"listUrl":"https://historicengland.org.uk/listing/the-list/list-entry/1255017","qid":"Q17553866","image":"http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Byron%20house%202.JPG","article":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arkwright_Building","cat":"Arkwright Building, Nottingham Trent University","stories":[{"layer":"lacemarket","text":"On the night of 8–9 May 1941 the Luftwaffe bombed Nottingham. Firefighters saved St Mary's Church after an incendiary burned through its roof; three Boots factories, in Poplar Street, Island Street and Station Street, were on fire; University College and the Shakespeare Street chapel were hit. The worst loss was at the Co-op bakery on Meadow Lane, where 49 people died. In all, 159 people were killed that night.","source":{"title":"Nottingham Blitz","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nottingham_Blitz","kind":"Wikipedia"},"nm":"The Nottingham Blitz, 1941"},{"layer":"north","text":"Built in 1877–81 for University College Nottingham, with the town's library and a natural history museum, and damaged by bombs in the Second World War. D. H. Lawrence studied here to become a teacher; it is now Nottingham Trent University's Arkwright Building.","source":{"title":"Arkwright Building","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arkwright_Building","kind":"Wikipedia"},"weblinks":[["D. H. Lawrence","note:D. H. Lawrence"]],"links":["D. H. Lawrence"],"nm":"University College","see":[["Trent Building","82807"]]},{"layer":"people","text":"Built between 1877 and 1881 for University College Nottingham, where D. H. Lawrence studied during his teacher-training years.[^s1]","source":{"title":"D. H. Lawrence","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/D._H._Lawrence","kind":"Wikipedia"},"who":"D. H. Lawrence","sources":[{"title":"Arkwright Building","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arkwright_Building","kind":"Wikipedia"}],"cites":{"s1":{"title":"Arkwright Building","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arkwright_Building","kind":"Wikipedia"}},"own":1}],"sum":"The Arkwright Building is a Grade II* listed university building on Shakespeare Street in Nottingham, England. It forms part of the Nottingham Trent University City Campus and is one of the university's principal historic buildings. Built between 1877 and 1881, the building was originally home to University College Nottingham, the city library and a natural history museum.","sumSrc":{"title":"Arkwright Building","kind":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arkwright_Building"},"council":"Nottingham City Council","coll":[{"title":"D. H. Lawrence","url":"https://picturenottingham.co.uk/image-themes/dh-lawrence.html"}],"xy":[-1.15244,52.95735]},"241948":{"h":10,"osm":"way/1004738507","kind":"office","stories":[{"layer":"stanns","text":"Edward VI granted the land on Hunger Hill to the Corporation of Nottingham in 1551. In 1604–05 it was enclosed and let in plots to 30 burgesses and their widows, an arrangement that lasted until 1842.","source":{"title":"St Ann's Allotments","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St_Ann%27s_Allotments","kind":"Wikipedia"},"when":"1551","whenSrc":{"title":"St Ann's Allotments","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St_Ann%27s_Allotments","kind":"Wikipedia"},"nm":"Hunger Hills"},{"layer":"stanns","text":"In the 1830s the Clay Fields were divided into plots, and the gardens on Hunger Hill became the St Ann's Allotments, the oldest and largest allotment site in England.[^s1] Hungerhill Gardens alone has 456 plots over about 52 acres, with sheds and summerhouses from the 19th century; the whole site is listed Grade II*.[^s2]","source":{"title":"St Ann's Allotments","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St_Ann%27s_Allotments","kind":"Wikipedia"},"sources":[{"title":"St Ann's, Nottingham","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St_Ann%27s,_Nottingham","kind":"Wikipedia"}],"cites":{"s1":{"title":"St Ann's, Nottingham","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St_Ann%27s,_Nottingham","kind":"Wikipedia"},"s2":{"title":"St Ann's Allotments","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St_Ann%27s_Allotments","kind":"Wikipedia"}},"when":"1830s","whenSrc":{"title":"St Ann's, Nottingham","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St_Ann%27s,_Nottingham","kind":"Wikipedia"},"nm":"St Ann's Allotments"}],"sum":"St Anns Allotments Visitor Centre is an office building on Ransom Road.","sumSrc":{"title":"OpenStreetMap","kind":"Written from the map's data"},"council":"Nottingham City Council","xy":[-1.13202,52.96984]},"298577":{"y":1860,"f":"approx","src":"OpenStreetMap","h":8,"osm":"way/272498079","kind":"terrace","listEntry":1270437,"listUrl":"https://historicengland.org.uk/listing/the-list/list-entry/1270437","qid":"Q26560495","stories":[{"layer":"stanns","text":"Until the Inclosure Act of 1845 the area was open Clay Fields. The Act let the town take 1,068 acres for housing, and by 1880 the streets of terraced houses called New Town were complete. The terraces north of Victoria Park are listed, as a once common kind of house now mostly lost to bombs and clearance.","source":{"title":"St Ann's, Nottingham","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St_Ann%27s,_Nottingham","kind":"Wikipedia"},"when":"1845","whenSrc":{"title":"St Ann's, Nottingham","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St_Ann%27s,_Nottingham","kind":"Wikipedia"},"nm":"New Town"}],"sum":"1 to 30, the Promenade is a terrace of houses on Promenade. The building dates from about 1860, according to OpenStreetMap. It is Grade II listed.","sumSrc":{"title":"OpenStreetMap · Historic England","kind":"Written from the map's data"},"council":"Nottingham City Council","xy":[-1.13845,52.95698]}}